Body:
Still reproduces on v2.0.10 (Windows 11 26200, PowerShell 7), and the trigger is broader than a missing `tools`: **any incomplete `capabilities` object drops the whole provider entry.**

In my case the V1 → V2 migration produced the mirror of your example — `tool_call: true` became `capabilities: { "tools": true }` with no `input`/`output`:

```jsonc
"models": {
  "k3": {
    "name": "Kimi K3",
    "capabilities": { "tools": true },
    "limit": { "context": 1048576, "output": 131072 }
  }
}
```

→ `path=$.providers.<provider> kind=invalid action="skipped malformed recognized value"`, provider absent from `/models`, and a session pinned to it failed with `Model unavailable: <provider>/k3`. Completing it to `{ "tools": true, "input": ["text"], "output": ["text"] }` makes the provider load immediately.

Two additional data points:

- **Not limited to custom providers.** A `providers.ollama` entry (recognized/built-in) with a `models` block using the same partial `capabilities` was skipped identically. Built-in Ollama discovery still supplied models, which masked the skipped config entry and made it much harder to spot.
- **The generic warning sends you down false trails.** Because the message names only the provider path, I assumed the `aisdk:@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` form from the migrate-v1 guide was rejected (both failing entries happened to use it). After completing `capabilities`, I verified that `aisdk:@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` and `@opencode/ai/providers/openai-compatible` both load and both serve live requests — the package form was never the problem. Naming the offending field would have made that obvious.

The models doc's fallback assumptions ("Tool support, text and image input, and text output" for non-catalog models) read as if a partial `capabilities` object should merge with those defaults rather than invalidate the entry.
